# Copy to .env (git-ignored) or export these in your pipeline.
#
# These describe a DEPLOYMENT, not a connector: which roles may query it, and where it goes. What a
# connector *is* — its handler class, its jar, its columns — lives in its own CDK stack, committed.
#
# Precedence: exported environment variables beat this file, and a connector's own
# <id>/cdk/.env beats this one. So a pipeline always wins, and one connector can override the rest.

# ── Required ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two COA components reach a connector through Athena, as different roles. Each is granted
# lambda:InvokeFunction on the function, s3:GetObject under its spill prefix, and kms:Decrypt on its
# spill key. Comma-separate either variable to serve more than one COA deployment; set both to the
# same value if one role does both jobs.
#
#   serve     - runs the queries.
#   discovery - runs DESCRIBE, which is how the @pk / @fk constraint tags are read at all. Omit it
#               and the connector answers SELECT perfectly while no declared key is ever found.
#
# Read both from SSM in COA's own account, where they are authoritative — the role names are
# generated, so do not try to guess them. {prefix} is the resource prefix COA was deployed with and
# {envName} is the COA environment, e.g. /coa/dev:
#   aws ssm get-parameter --name /{prefix}/{envName}/serve/runtime-role-arn        --query Parameter.Value
#   aws ssm get-parameter --name /{prefix}/{envName}/sources/db-connector-role-arn --query Parameter.Value
#
# Pasted here rather than read by the connector on purpose: a connector normally runs in a different
# account from COA, and SSM parameters are not readable across accounts.
SERVE_ROLE_ARN=arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/<the value of that SSM parameter>
DISCOVERY_ROLE_ARN=arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/<the value of that SSM parameter>

# The connector's target region. EXPORT IT IN YOUR SHELL — a value here is ignored whenever you
# deploy through the CDK CLI, which sets CDK_DEFAULT_REGION from the active profile on every run,
# and that wins. Left here commented as documentation only.
#
#   AWS_REGION=<region> pnpm run deploy
#
# Deploying into the wrong region succeeds and leaves a connector nothing will ever query, so check
# the region in the synth output before you deploy.
# AWS_REGION=eu-central-1

# ── Optional ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Target account. Left unset, CDK resolves it from the active AWS profile and the stack becomes
# environment-agnostic. CDK_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT wins over this.
# AWS_ACCOUNT_ID=111122223333

# Prefixes the Lambda and stack names, for telling two deployments of the same connector apart when
# they share an account — two COA environments, or two instances of one source. Permissions do not
# depend on it: COA scopes invoke on the coa:connector tag, not on the function's name.
# FUNCTION_NAME_PREFIX=acme-dev-

# Example connector only: size its bulk_rows fixture past Athena's 6 MB response limit to exercise
# the S3 spill path. Both default small, so nothing spills unless you ask.
# EXAMPLE_BULK_ROWS=4096
# EXAMPLE_BULK_ROW_BYTES=2048

# ── Databricks connector ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The four below are REQUIRED to deploy databricks/cdk, and its synth fails naming whichever is
# missing. One deployment serves one workspace, one warehouse and one Unity Catalog catalog. See
# databricks/README.md.
#
# DATABRICKS_WORKSPACE_HOSTNAME=dbc-a1b2345c-d6e7.cloud.databricks.com   # no scheme, no port
#   AWS .cloud.databricks.com, Azure .azuredatabricks.net, GCP .gcp.databricks.com
# DATABRICKS_HTTP_PATH=/sql/1.0/warehouses/a1b234c567d8e9fa             # Connection details tab
# DATABRICKS_CATALOG=main
#
# OPTIONAL, and a security control when set. Pins the connector to ONE Unity Catalog schema: it
# then refuses a query naming any other, whatever the credential's UC grants allow. Leave it unset
# and one connector serves EVERY schema in DATABRICKS_CATALOG, with COA's databaseName selecting
# which schema each source exposes — convenient, but the credential's own grants become the only
# boundary, so scope that credential to exactly the schemas you intend to expose.
# DATABRICKS_SCHEMA=sales
#
# A POINTER to the credential, never the credential. The secret's JSON shape selects the auth mode:
# {"token": ...} for a personal access token, {"client_id": ..., "client_secret": ...} for OAuth
# machine-to-machine, which is the mode to prefer. A secret carrying both is refused.
# CREDENTIAL_SECRET_ARN=arn:aws:secretsmanager:us-east-1:111122223333:secret:dbx-AbCdEf
#
# Only when that secret is encrypted with a customer-managed key. No existing grant covers it, and
# without it every read fails with access-denied at run time rather than at deploy.
# CREDENTIAL_KMS_KEY_ARN=arn:aws:kms:us-east-1:111122223333:key/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
#
# Rows one table may return before the connector fails with an error naming it. Defaults to
# 2,000,000. Raise it together with the function's memory and timeout.
# DATABRICKS_MAX_ROWS_PER_TABLE=2000000
#
# Runs the function in your VPC. Both or neither: private subnets with egress, comma-separated. The
# subnets need a route to the workspace (NAT, or a Databricks PrivateLink endpoint, which is the only
# way to reach a PrivateLink-only workspace) and to Secrets Manager, KMS and S3. A
# missing route fails the first query, not the deploy. See databricks/README.md.
# CONNECTOR_VPC_ID=vpc-0a1b2c3d4e5f60718
# CONNECTOR_SUBNET_IDS=subnet-0a1b2c3d4e5f60711,subnet-0a1b2c3d4e5f60722
#
# Your own security groups. Unset, the stack creates one with HTTPS egress only.
# CONNECTOR_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS=sg-0a1b2c3d4e5f60733
